151 0 obj
<</Type/StructElem/S/H3/P 35 0 R/T(1. Types: 10 is int, 10.0 is float, '10' is str \(text in quotes\), True is bool, [10, 8.5] is list. 2. Operators: 7 / 2 = 3.5 \(true division always gives float\); 7 // 2 = 3 \(floor division\); 7 % 2 = 1 \(remainder\); -7 // 2 = -4 because floor division rounds down toward minus infinity \(-3.5 becomes -4, not -3\).)/E(1. Types: 10 is int, 10.0 is float, '10' is str \(text in quotes\), True is bool, [10, 8.5] is list. 2. Operators: 7 / 2 = 3.5 \(true division always gives float\); 7 // 2 = 3 \(floor division\); 7 % 2 = 1 \(remainder\); -7 // 2 = -4 because floor division rounds down toward minus infinity \(-3.5 becomes -4, not -3\).)/Pg 11 0 R/K[31]>>
endobj

152 0 obj
<</Type/StructElem/S/H3/P 35 0 R/T(3. 0.1 + 0.2 == 0.3 is False. Computers store floats in binary. 0.1 and 0.2 cannot be written exactly in binary \(like 1/3 in decimal\), so each is stored as the nearest binary fraction. Their sum is 0.30000000000000004, which is not the same float as 0.3. With Decimal\('0.1'\) + Decimal\('0.2'\) the digits are stored in decimal, so the result is exactly 0.3 and the comparison is True.)/E(3. 0.1 + 0.2 == 0.3 is False. Computers store floats in binary. 0.1 and 0.2 cannot be written exactly in binary \(like 1/3 in decimal\), so each is stored as the nearest binary fraction. Their sum is 0.30000000000000004, which is not the same float as 0.3. With Decimal\('0.1'\) + Decimal\('0.2'\) the digits are stored in decimal, so the result is exactly 0.3 and the comparison is True.)/Pg 11 0 R/K[32]>>
endobj

153 0 obj
<</Type/StructElem/S/H3/P 35 0 R/T(4. Why floats are risky for money: prices such as 33.40 are not exactly representable, and every addition can add a tiny error. Over 1,003 additions the float total was 33400.30000000061 instead of 33400.30. The error is small, but accounts must match to the paisa, equality checks fail, and errors can change rounded totals.)/E(4. Why floats are risky for money: prices such as 33.40 are not exactly representable, and every addition can add a tiny error. Over 1,003 additions the float total was 33400.30000000061 instead of 33400.30. The error is small, but accounts must match to the paisa, equality checks fail, and errors can change rounded totals.)/Pg 11 0 R/K[33]>>
endobj

154 0 obj
<</Type/StructElem/S/H3/P 35 0 R/T(5. Comparison: integer paise \(store 33.40 as 3340\): exact and fast, but you must convert in and out and be careful with multiplication by rates. round\(\) on floats: hides the error when printing/rounding, but the stored numbers are still)/E(5. Comparison: integer paise \(store 33.40 as 3340\): exact and fast, but you must convert in and out and be careful with multiplication by rates. round\(\) on floats: hides the error when printing/rounding, but the stored numbers are still)/Pg 11 0 R/K[34]>>
endobj

