575 0 obj
<</Type/StructElem/S/H2/P 41 0 R/T(1. Parameters/returns: rate_per_litre\(fat, snf\) returns the rate; payout\(litres, fat, snf\) returns the payment; ten_day_total\(entries\) returns the total for a list of \(litres, fat, snf\). 2. Return vs print: return hands the value back to the caller so it can be stored, used in other calculations or tested; print only shows text on the screen and the function then returns None \(output: 'print-only function gives: None'\). 3. Local vs global: variables created inside a function \(fat_steps, snf_steps, total\) are local - they exist only while it runs \(accessing fat_steps outside gives NameError, shown above\). Names defined at module level \(BASE_RATE ...\) are global. 4. Constants: a function can read a global constant without any special keyword. Assigning to the name inside a function would create a new local variable \(or need the 'global' keyword to change the real one\); changing constants inside functions is dangerous because it silently changes the result of every other function that uses the same constant and is hard to debug. 5. Docstrings are given in every function. Additional question - long script vs functions vs lambda \(n = 100,000 entries\))/E(1. Parameters/returns: rate_per_litre\(fat, snf\) returns the rate; payout\(litres, fat, snf\) returns the payment; ten_day_total\(entries\) returns the total for a list of \(litres, fat, snf\). 2. Return vs print: return hands the value back to the caller so it can be stored, used in other calculations or tested; print only shows text on the screen and the function then returns None \(output: 'print-only function gives: None'\). 3. Local vs global: variables created inside a function \(fat_steps, snf_steps, total\) are local - they exist only while it runs \(accessing fat_steps outside gives NameError, shown above\). Names defined at module level \(BASE_RATE ...\) are global. 4. Constants: a function can read a global constant without any special keyword. Assigning to the name inside a function would create a new local variable \(or need the 'global' keyword to change the real one\); changing constants inside functions is dangerous because it silently changes the result of every other function that uses the same constant and is hard to debug. 5. Docstrings are given in every function. Additional question - long script vs functions vs lambda \(n = 100,000 entries\))/Pg 17 0 R/K[7]>>
endobj

