699 0 obj
<</Type/StructElem/S/H3/P 43 0 R/T(time another script imports the module - unwanted output and side effects. That is why the table is not printed when milkrate is imported.)/E(time another script imports the module - unwanted output and side effects. That is why the table is not printed when milkrate is imported.)/Pg 19 0 R/K[11]>>
endobj

700 0 obj
<</Type/StructElem/S/H3/P 43 0 R/T(4. Standard modules: math gives functions such as floor\(\), isfinite\(\); decimal gives the exact Decimal type and rounding modes. They are installed with Python, imported with 'import math' / 'from decimal import Decimal'. 5. Module search: Python looks for a module in sys.modules \(already imported\), then in the folder of the running script \(sys.path[0]\), then the other folders listed in sys.path \(PYTHONPATH, standard library, site-packages\) - the first match wins. So milkrate.py must be in the same folder as the script \(or in a folder on sys.path\), and you should not name your file like a standard module \(e.g. math.py\) because it would be found first.)/E(4. Standard modules: math gives functions such as floor\(\), isfinite\(\); decimal gives the exact Decimal type and rounding modes. They are installed with Python, imported with 'import math' / 'from decimal import Decimal'. 5. Module search: Python looks for a module in sys.modules \(already imported\), then in the folder of the running script \(sys.path[0]\), then the other folders listed in sys.path \(PYTHONPATH, standard library, site-packages\) - the first match wins. So milkrate.py must be in the same folder as the script \(or in a folder on sys.path\), and you should not name your file like a standard module \(e.g. math.py\) because it would be found first.)/Pg 19 0 R/K[12]>>
endobj

701 0 obj
<</Type/StructElem/S/H2/P 43 0 R/T(Additional question - import vs copying the functions into every script)/E(Additional question - import vs copying the functions into every script)/Pg 19 0 R/K[13]>>
endobj

702 0 obj
<</Type/StructElem/S/H3/P 43 0 R/T(Maintainability/rate revision: with a module, a rate revision is made in one file \(one place\) and every script gets it; with copies the same edit must be repeated in every script and a missed copy gives different payments for the same milk. One-time cost of importing: the first import finds, compiles \(to .pyc\) and executes the module once; this costs a little time \(typically fractions of a millisecond to a few milliseconds\) but happens only once per program run. sys.modules: Python keeps a dictionary of every imported module. After the first import the module object is stored in sys.modules \(the output shows 'milkrate in sys.modules: True'\); any later 'import milkrate' just returns the stored object without reading or running the file again, so repeated imports are almost free.)/E(Maintainability/rate revision: with a module, a rate revision is made in one file \(one place\) and every script gets it; with copies the same edit must be repeated in every script and a missed copy gives different payments for the same milk. One-time cost of importing: the first import finds, compiles \(to .pyc\) and executes the module once; this costs a little time \(typically fractions of a millisecond to a few milliseconds\) but happens only once per program run. sys.modules: Python keeps a dictionary of every imported module. After the first import the module object is stored in sys.modules \(the output shows 'milkrate in sys.modules: True'\); any later 'import milkrate' just returns the stored object without reading or running the file again, so repeated imports are almost free.)/Pg 19 0 R/K[14]>>
endobj

