<</Type/StructElem/S/H2/P 47 0 R/T(Expected = the exact decimal total; actual = the float total. They differ in the 10th-14th significant digits because each float addition rounds to the nearest binary value, and these tiny errors accumulate over more additions \(differences of about 1e-10 for 300, 1e-8 for 6,000, and 5e-7 for 150,000 payments\). Decimal is exact but about 1.1-1.9 times slower; even for 150,000 payments it takes only about 31 ms, so the speed cost is not a problem.)/E(Expected = the exact decimal total; actual = the float total. They differ in the 10th-14th significant digits because each float addition rounds to the nearest binary value, and these tiny errors accumulate over more additions \(differences of about 1e-10 for 300, 1e-8 for 6,000, and 5e-7 for 150,000 payments\). Decimal is exact but about 1.1-1.9 times slower; even for 150,000 payments it takes only about 31 ms, so the speed cost is not a problem.)/Pg 23 0 R/K[26]>>
endobj

1034 0 obj
<</Type/StructElem/S/H1/P 47 0 R/T(Round each payment, or only the final total?)/E(Round each payment, or only the final total?)/Pg 23 0 R/K[27]>>
endobj

1035 0 obj
<</Type/StructElem/S/H2/P 47 0 R/T(A farmer can only be paid a whole number of paise, so the bank really transfers the sum of the individually rounded payments. Rounding only the final total gives a different number \(about Rs 19.58 less for 150,000 payments in this experiment\), which would not match what was actually paid to the farmers. Therefore round every payment to 2 decimals \(preferably with Decimal\) and add the rounded values. \(The test payments here are synthetic, with 4 decimal places before rounding; the point - rounding position changes the total - holds for real data.\))/E(A farmer can only be paid a whole number of paise, so the bank really transfers the sum of the individually rounded payments. Rounding only the final total gives a different number \(about Rs 19.58 less for 150,000 payments in this experiment\), which would not match what was actually paid to the farmers. Therefore round every payment to 2 decimals \(preferably with Decimal\) and add the rounded values. \(The test payments here are synthetic, with 4 decimal places before rounding; the point - rounding position changes the total - holds for real data.\))/Pg 23 0 R/K[28]>>
endobj

1036 0 obj
<</Type/StructElem/S/H1/P 47 0 R/T(Additional question - rate table \(dictionary\) vs recalculation)/E(Additional question - rate table \(dictionary\) vs recalculation)/Pg 23 0 R/K[29]>>
endobj

1037 0 obj
<</Type/StructElem/S/H2/P 47 0 R/T(How it works: compute the rate once for every possible \(fat, snf\) pair and store it in a dictionary keyed by the pair; then each entry just looks the rate up: rate = table[\(fat, snf\)]. With 0.1% resolution there are 51 fat values \(2.0-7.0\) x 31 SNF values \(7.0-10.0\) = k = 1,581 pairs. Time per entry: recalculation about 493 ns; dictionary lookup about 91 ns \(O\(1\) average\) - roughly 5 times faster. Building the table costs about 1.0 ms once \(O\(k\)\); it pays off after roughly 2,500 entries \(1.0 ms divided by the ~0.4 microsecond saved per entry\). Extra space: O\(k\) - the dictionary structure alone was 73,808 bytes for k = 1,581 \(plus the key and value objects\). Limitation: it only works when fat/SNF come from a small finite set; with the 2-decimal analyser readings of Task 12 there would be about 501 x 301 = 150,801 pairs, so the table becomes large and recalculation is better.)/E(How it works: compute the rate once for every possible \(fat, snf\) pair and store it in a dictionary keyed by the pair; then each entry just looks the rate up: rate = table[\(fat, snf\)]. With 0.1% resolution there are 51 fat values \(2.0-7.0\) x 31 SNF values \(7.0-10.0\) = k = 1,581 pairs. Time per entry: recalculation about 493 ns; dictionary lookup about 91 ns \(O\(1\) average\) - roughly 5 times faster. Building the table costs about 1.0 ms once \(O\(k\)\); it pays off after roughly 2,500 entries \(1.0 ms divided by the ~0.4 microsecond saved per entry\). Extra space: O\(k\) - the dictionary structure alone was 73,808 bytes for k = 1,581 \(plus the key and value objects\). Limitation: it only works when fat/SNF come from a small finite set; with the 2-decimal analyser readings of Task 12 there would be about 501 x 301 = 150,801 pairs, so the table becomes large and recalculation is better.)/Pg 23 0 R/K[30]>>
endobj

