<</Type/StructElem/S/H3/P 49 0 R/T(3. Limitations: the SNF range \(7.0-10.0\) is my assumption; the step rule \(nearest whole step\) loses information if readings have more decimals \(see Task 12\); Decimal is slower than float \(about 2.5 microseconds per entry measured\); all data is processed in memory with no database or audit trail; the ten-day period is assumed not to cross a rate-change date.)/E(3. Limitations: the SNF range \(7.0-10.0\) is my assumption; the step rule \(nearest whole step\) loses information if readings have more decimals \(see Task 12\); Decimal is slower than float \(about 2.5 microseconds per entry measured\); all data is processed in memory with no database or audit trail; the ten-day period is assumed not to cross a rate-change date.)/Pg 25 0 R/K[12]>>
endobj

1147 0 obj
<</Type/StructElem/S/H3/P 49 0 R/T(4. Can it support 150,000 farmers? Yes for computation. Measured: 150,000 entries are priced in about 0.39 s \(2.6 microseconds per entry, growing linearly: 300 -> 0.8 ms, 6,000 -> 14.6 ms\). One ten-day cycle for 150,000 farmers is about 150,000 x 20 = 3,000,000 entries, estimated by linear extrapolation at about 8 seconds, which is acceptable for a batch job. The weak point is memory if all 3,000,000 entries are loaded as Decimal tuples at once \(rough estimate: more than 1 GB\), so the data should be read and processed in a stream \(file/database row by row, running total per farmer\), as in Task 8.)/E(4. Can it support 150,000 farmers? Yes for computation. Measured: 150,000 entries are priced in about 0.39 s \(2.6 microseconds per entry, growing linearly: 300 -> 0.8 ms, 6,000 -> 14.6 ms\). One ten-day cycle for 150,000 farmers is about 150,000 x 20 = 3,000,000 entries, estimated by linear extrapolation at about 8 seconds, which is acceptable for a batch job. The weak point is memory if all 3,000,000 entries are loaded as Decimal tuples at once \(rough estimate: more than 1 GB\), so the data should be read and processed in a stream \(file/database row by row, running total per farmer\), as in Task 8.)/Pg 25 0 R/K[13]>>
endobj

1148 0 obj
<</Type/StructElem/S/H3/P 49 0 R/T(5. Justification from experiments: Task 2 and 10 showed float totals drift \(e.g. 33400.30000000061\) while Decimal is exact; Task 10 showed that rounding each payment matches the real bank transfer; Task 9 tests pass; the scalability test above shows linear time; Task 7's module design means a rate change is made in one place.)/E(5. Justification from experiments: Task 2 and 10 showed float totals drift \(e.g. 33400.30000000061\) while Decimal is exact; Task 10 showed that rounding each payment matches the real bank transfer; Task 9 tests pass; the scalability test above shows linear time; Task 7's module design means a rate change is made in one place.)/Pg 25 0 R/K[14]>>
endobj

1149 0 obj
<</Type/StructElem/S/H2/P 49 0 R/T(Additional question - rate card stored in a text/JSON file loaded at start-up)/E(Additional question - rate card stored in a text/JSON file loaded at start-up)/Pg 25 0 R/K[15]>>
endobj

1150 0 obj
<</Type/StructElem/S/H3/P 49 0 R/T(How it works: the rates are kept in a file such as {'base': '30.00', 'fat_step': '0.40', 'snf_step': '0.30'}; at start-up the program reads the file with json.load\(\) into a dictionary and converts the strings to Decimal. Start-up time: O\(t\) for t rate values - measured 1.8 microseconds for 3 values, 0.2 ms for 1,000 and about 30 ms for 100,000. Space: O\(t\) \(the 100,000-value file was about 1.8 MB\). Maintenance: rates can be revised by editing the file with no change to Python code and no redistribution of the program after each rate revision; the cost is that the file can be missing or malformed, so the program must validate it when loading \(store numbers as strings or use parse_float=Decimal so no float errors are introduced\). For the few rate values here the load cost is negligible, so file-based rates are a good improvement for maintainability.)/E(How it works: the rates are kept in a file such as {'base': '30.00', 'fat_step': '0.40', 'snf_step': '0.30'}; at start-up the program reads the file with json.load\(\) into a dictionary and converts the strings to Decimal. Start-up time: O\(t\) for t rate values - measured 1.8 microseconds for 3 values, 0.2 ms for 1,000 and about 30 ms for 100,000. Space: O\(t\) \(the 100,000-value file was about 1.8 MB\). Maintenance: rates can be revised by editing the file with no change to Python code and no redistribution of the program after each rate revision; the cost is that the file can be missing or malformed, so the program must validate it when loading \(store numbers as strings or use parse_float=Decimal so no float errors are introduced\). For the few rate values here the load cost is negligible, so file-based rates are a good improvement for maintainability.)/Pg 25 0 R/K[16]>>
endobj

