1350 0 obj
<</Title(Separate variables: 300 farmers need 900 hand-written names \(litres1...litres300, fat1..., snf1...\) and the calculation code has to be repeated or generated for each name, because a loop cannot step through variable names. Adding a farmer means editing the source code. Source-code size grows as O\(n\). List/tuple of records: one name holds all entries; one loop processes any n; source code size is O\(1\) and data is added at run time.)/Dest[11 0 R/Fit]/Parent 1306 0 R/Next 1351 0 R>>
endobj

1351 0 obj
<</Title(Complexity for n entries: processing time is O\(n\) for both \(each entry must be computed once\); memory for the data is O\(n\) for both. The difference is in code size/maintainability \(O\(n\) lines vs O\(1\)\) and in the fact that the list can be looped, sorted, filtered and validated with the same code. The list/tuple approach is far more scalable: for 6,000 or 150,000 farmers, separate variables would need 18,000 or 450,000 names, which is impractical.)/Dest[11 0 R/Fit]/Parent 1306 0 R/Prev 1350 0 R>>
endobj

1352 0 obj
<</Title(Annotated session \(task02_session.py evaluates each expression exactly as the >>> prompt would\):)/Dest[11 0 R/Fit]/Parent 1307 0 R/Next 1427 0 R>>
endobj

1353 0 obj
<</Title(approximations and round\(\) uses 'round half to even'. decimal module: exact decimal arithmetic with a chosen rounding rule; slightly slower and uses more memory, but the safest for money.)/Dest[12 0 R/Fit]/Parent 1307 0 R/Prev 1427 0 R>>
endobj

