1538 0 obj
<</Title(2. Outputs)/Dest[10 0 R/Fit]/Parent 1348 0 R/Prev 1423 0 R/Next 1606 0 R>>
endobj

1539 0 obj
<</Title(4. Classification of values)/Dest[10 0 R/Fit]/Parent 1348 0 R/Prev 1606 0 R/First 1607 0 R/Last 1607 0 R/Count -1/Next 1424 0 R>>
endobj

1540 0 obj
<</Title(\(a\) litres >= 0 \(zero allowed\). \(b\) 2.0 <= fat <= 7.0. \(c\) 7.0 <= snf <= 10.0 \(assumed range\). \(d\) farmer ID not empty. \(e\) shift must be 'morning' or 'evening'. \(f\) litres, fat and SNF must be valid numbers \(not text such as 'eight' or '8,5', and not nan/inf\).)/Dest[10 0 R/Fit]/Parent 1424 0 R>>
endobj

1541 0 obj
<</Title(1. Types: 10 is int, 10.0 is float, '10' is str \(text in quotes\), True is bool, [10, 8.5] is list. 2. Operators: 7 / 2 = 3.5 \(true division always gives float\); 7 // 2 = 3 \(floor division\); 7 % 2 = 1 \(remainder\); -7 // 2 = -4 because floor division rounds down toward minus infinity \(-3.5 becomes -4, not -3\).)/Dest[11 0 R/Fit]/Parent 1427 0 R/Next 1608 0 R>>
endobj

1542 0 obj
<</Title(5. Comparison: integer paise \(store 33.40 as 3340\): exact and fast, but you must convert in and out and be careful with multiplication by rates. round\(\) on floats: hides the error when printing/rounding, but the stored numbers are still)/Dest[11 0 R/Fit]/Parent 1427 0 R/Prev 1609 0 R/First 1610 0 R/Last 1611 0 R/Count -2>>
endobj

1543 0 obj
<</Title(Result: 33400.29999999999857893118278 - wrong. float\('33.40'\) is already the inexact binary value 33.39999999999999857891..., and Decimal\(float\) copies that inexact value exactly. So the float error is imported into the Decimal. Decimal is only exact if it is created from a string \(Decimal\('33.40'\)\) or an integer. \(Decimal prints 28 significant digits by default.\))/Dest[12 0 R/Fit]/Parent 1429 0 R>>
endobj

1544 0 obj
<</Title(Additional question - Decimal vs integer paise vs float with rounding after every addition)/Dest[12 0 R/Fit]/Parent 7 0 R/Prev 1429 0 R/First 1612 0 R/Last 1613 0 R/Count -9/Next 1614 0 R>>
endobj

1545 0 obj
<</Title(Additional question - rate table \(dictionary\) vs recalculation)/Dest[23 0 R/Fit]/Parent 7 0 R/Prev 1615 0 R/First 1616 0 R/Last 1616 0 R/Count -1/Next 1430 0 R>>
endobj

1546 0 obj
<</Title(Output \(task11_evaluation.py\):)/Dest[24 0 R/Fit]/Parent 1430 0 R/Next 1617 0 R>>
endobj

1547 0 obj
<</Title(Additional question - rate card stored in a text/JSON file loaded at start-up)/Dest[25 0 R/Fit]/Parent 1430 0 R/Prev 1617 0 R/First 1618 0 R/Last 1618 0 R/Count -1>>
endobj

1548 0 obj
<</Title(1. What changed: \(i\) the analyser gives fat and SNF with two decimal places \(e.g. 3.77%\), so readings are finer than the 0.1% step; \(ii\) from April to June the base rate is Rs 32.00 and each fat step is worth Rs 0.45 \(SNF step unchanged at 0.30\); \(iii\) a farmer supplying more than 500 litres in ten days receives a bonus of Rs 0.50 per litre.)/Dest[25 0 R/Fit]/Parent 1431 0 R/Next 1619 0 R>>
endobj

1549 0 obj
<</Title(3. Effect of two-decimal readings on steps and rounding: step counts become fractional \(0.27 / 0.1 = 2.7\). If the old nearest-whole-step rule is kept, nearby readings get the same price and prices are off by up to half a step \(Rs 0.20/litre for fat\), and exact ties \(x.5 steps\) arise. Float arithmetic also makes these fractional steps inexact, so Decimal is needed. Rates such as 33.605 need an explicit rounding rule \(half up, to paise\).)/Dest[25 0 R/Fit]/Parent 1431 0 R/Prev 1619 0 R>>
endobj

1550 0 obj
<</Title(B. Evaluation of the original design \(worked examples with the new readings, normal rate card\))/Dest[25 0 R/Fit]/Parent 1355 0 R/Prev 1431 0 R/First 1620 0 R/Last 1621 0 R/Count -3/Next 1432 0 R>>
endobj

1551 0 obj
<</Title(CSA08 Python Programming - Assignment 1 - Shaik Babji)/Dest[25 0 R/Fit]/Parent 1432 0 R/Next 1622 0 R>>
endobj

1552 0 obj
<</Title(SUMMER_MONTHS = \(4, 5, 6\))/Dest[26 0 R/Fit]/Parent 1432 0 R/Prev 1622 0 R>>
endobj

