1606 0 obj
<</Title(3. Values that should be named constants)/Dest[10 0 R/Fit]/Parent 1348 0 R/Prev 1538 0 R/First 1675 0 R/Last 1676 0 R/Count -5/Next 1539 0 R>>
endobj

1607 0 obj
<</Title(Integers: number of days \(10\), step counts \(fat_steps, snf_steps\), number of farmers \(300\). Floating-point numbers: litres, fat %, SNF %, rate, payment \(or Decimal for exact money\). Booleans: validity result of an entry \(True/False\), result of comparisons such as 2.0 <= fat <= 7.0. Strings: farmer ID, shift.)/Dest[10 0 R/Fit]/Parent 1539 0 R>>
endobj

1608 0 obj
<</Title(3. 0.1 + 0.2 == 0.3 is False. Computers store floats in binary. 0.1 and 0.2 cannot be written exactly in binary \(like 1/3 in decimal\), so each is stored as the nearest binary fraction. Their sum is 0.30000000000000004, which is not the same float as 0.3. With Decimal\('0.1'\) + Decimal\('0.2'\) the digits are stored in decimal, so the result is exactly 0.3 and the comparison is True.)/Dest[11 0 R/Fit]/Parent 1427 0 R/Prev 1541 0 R/Next 1609 0 R>>
endobj

1609 0 obj
<</Title(4. Why floats are risky for money: prices such as 33.40 are not exactly representable, and every addition can add a tiny error. Over 1,003 additions the float total was 33400.30000000061 instead of 33400.30. The error is small, but accounts must match to the paisa, equality checks fail, and errors can change rounded totals.)/Dest[11 0 R/Fit]/Parent 1427 0 R/Prev 1608 0 R/Next 1542 0 R>>
endobj

1610 0 obj
<</Title(CSA08 Python Programming - Assignment 1 - Shaik Babji)/Dest[11 0 R/Fit]/Parent 1542 0 R/Next 1611 0 R>>
endobj

1611 0 obj
<</Title(Page 2)/Dest[11 0 R/Fit]/Parent 1542 0 R/Prev 1610 0 R>>
endobj

1612 0 obj
<</Title(Method)/Dest[12 0 R/Fit]/Parent 1544 0 R/Next 1677 0 R>>
endobj

1613 0 obj
<</Title(For n payments every method is O\(n\) time overall with O\(1\) extra space; the differences are only constant factors. Decimal or integer paise are the exact choices; float is not.)/Dest[12 0 R/Fit]/Parent 1544 0 R/Prev 1678 0 R/First 1679 0 R/Last 1680 0 R/Count -2>>
endobj

1614 0 obj
<</Title(TASK 3 - Choose Variables and Name the Constants)/Dest[13 0 R/Fit]/Parent 7 0 R/Prev 1544 0 R/First 1681 0 R/Last 1682 0 R/Count -4/Next 1683 0 R>>
endobj

1615 0 obj
<</Title(Round each payment, or only the final total?)/Dest[23 0 R/Fit]/Parent 7 0 R/Prev 1684 0 R/First 1685 0 R/Last 1685 0 R/Count -1/Next 1545 0 R>>
endobj

1616 0 obj
<</Title(How it works: compute the rate once for every possible \(fat, snf\) pair and store it in a dictionary keyed by the pair; then each entry just looks the rate up: rate = table[\(fat, snf\)]. With 0.1% resolution there are 51 fat values \(2.0-7.0\) x 31 SNF values \(7.0-10.0\) = k = 1,581 pairs. Time per entry: recalculation about 493 ns; dictionary lookup about 91 ns \(O\(1\) average\) - roughly 5 times faster. Building the table costs about 1.0 ms once \(O\(k\)\); it pays off after roughly 2,500 entries \(1.0 ms divided by the ~0.4 microsecond saved per entry\). Extra space: O\(k\) - the dictionary structure alone was 73,808 bytes for k = 1,581 \(plus the key and value objects\). Limitation: it only works when fat/SNF come from a small finite set; with the 2-decimal analyser readings of Task 12 there would be about 501 x 301 = 150,801 pairs, so the table becomes large and recalculation is better.)/Dest[23 0 R/Fit]/Parent 1545 0 R>>
endobj

1617 0 obj
<</Title(Recommended design)/Dest[24 0 R/Fit]/Parent 1430 0 R/Prev 1546 0 R/First 1686 0 R/Last 1687 0 R/Count -5/Next 1547 0 R>>
endobj

1618 0 obj
<</Title(How it works: the rates are kept in a file such as {'base': '30.00', 'fat_step': '0.40', 'snf_step': '0.30'}; at start-up the program reads the file with json.load\(\) into a dictionary and converts the strings to Decimal. Start-up time: O\(t\) for t rate values - measured 1.8 microseconds for 3 values, 0.2 ms for 1,000 and about 30 ms for 100,000. Space: O\(t\) \(the 100,000-value file was about 1.8 MB\). Maintenance: rates can be revised by editing the file with no change to Python code and no redistribution of the program after each rate revision; the cost is that the file can be missing or malformed, so the program must validate it when loading \(store numbers as strings or use parse_float=Decimal so no float errors are introduced\). For the few rate values here the load cost is negligible, so file-based rates are a good improvement for maintainability.)/Dest[25 0 R/Fit]/Parent 1547 0 R>>
endobj

1619 0 obj
<</Title(2. What must be modified: Constants: one rate card is no longer enough - two cards \(normal and April-June\) plus BONUS_THRESHOLD_LITRES = 500 and BONUS_PER_LITRE = 0.50. Expressions: step counts can no longer be rounded to a whole step \(3.77% is 2.7 steps\). Functions: rate_per_litre and payout need the rate card as a parameter; a new select_rate_card\(month\) and bonus\(total_litres\) are needed; ten_day_total must add the bonus; validation must accept 2-decimal readings.)/Dest[25 0 R/Fit]/Parent 1431 0 R/Prev 1548 0 R/Next 1549 0 R>>
endobj

1620 0 obj
<</Title(Fat 3.77 3.54 4.25 3.50)/Dest[25 0 R/Fit]/Parent 1550 0 R/Next 1688 0 R>>
endobj

1621 0 obj
<</Title(Still appropriate: named constants \(concept\), the separate module, the small functions \(rate_per_litre, payout, ten_day_total\), range validation, rounding each payment, the automated tests. No longer appropriate: a single set of rate constants, whole-step rounding, float arithmetic, and a ten_day_total without bonus/season. The design structure is sound, so a modification \(not a complete redesign\) is justified.)/Dest[25 0 R/Fit]/Parent 1550 0 R/Prev 1688 0 R>>
endobj

1622 0 obj
<</Title(Page 16)/Dest[25 0 R/Fit]/Parent 1432 0 R/Prev 1551 0 R/Next 1552 0 R>>
endobj

