endobj

1685 0 obj
<</Title(A farmer can only be paid a whole number of paise, so the bank really transfers the sum of the individually rounded payments. Rounding only the final total gives a different number \(about Rs 19.58 less for 150,000 payments in this experiment\), which would not match what was actually paid to the farmers. Therefore round every payment to 2 decimals \(preferably with Decimal\) and add the rounded values. \(The test payments here are synthetic, with 4 decimal places before rounding; the point - rounding position changes the total - holds for real data.\))/Dest[23 0 R/Fit]/Parent 1615 0 R>>
endobj

1686 0 obj
<</Title(1. Data types and rounding: use Decimal created from strings for fat %, SNF %, rates and money \(or integer paise\), integers for step counts and days, str for IDs and shift. Round every payment to paise with ROUND_HALF_UP \(the usual commercial rule\), then add the rounded payments \(Task 10\). Keep the rate card as named data \(module constants / dictionary\) separate from the formulas.)/Dest[24 0 R/Fit]/Parent 1617 0 R/First 1705 0 R/Last 1706 0 R/Count -2/Next 1707 0 R>>
endobj

1687 0 obj
<</Title(5. Justification from experiments: Task 2 and 10 showed float totals drift \(e.g. 33400.30000000061\) while Decimal is exact; Task 10 showed that rounding each payment matches the real bank transfer; Task 9 tests pass; the scalability test above shows linear time; Task 7's module design means a rate change is made in one place.)/Dest[25 0 R/Fit]/Parent 1617 0 R/Prev 1708 0 R>>
endobj

