<</Title(Python has no 'const' keyword: any name can be reassigned. The output above shows BASE_RATE = 99 was accepted without any error. The convention \(PEP 8\) of writing constants in UPPER_CASE tells every programmer 'do not change this value', lets reviewers and tools \(linters\) spot accidental changes, and makes the code self-documenting. The protection is by agreement, not by the language.)/Dest[13 0 R/Fit]/Parent 1697 0 R>>
endobj

1719 0 obj
<</Title(A Python variable is just a name that refers to an object; it has no fixed type. x = 10 makes x an int, and x = 'ten' later makes it a str \(shown in the output\). Multiple assignment a, b = 4.2, 8.7 assigns both in one statement, and a, b = b, a swaps them without a temporary variable, because the right side is fully evaluated first.)/Dest[13 0 R/Fit]/Parent 1698 0 R>>
endobj

