1741 0 obj
<</Title(\(4.2 - 3.5\) / 0.1: 4.2 and 3.5 and 0.1 are inexact in binary, so the result on my machine is 7.000000000000002 \(the assignment mentions a similar 7.000000000000001-type result\) instead of 7. Using round\(\) gives the nearest whole step count \(7\), so the tiny error does not matter.)/Dest[14 0 R/Fit]/Parent 1702 0 R/Prev 1723 0 R/Next 1742 0 R>>
endobj

1742 0 obj
<</Title(round\(\) vs int\(\) vs math.floor\(\) for negative values: round\(\) goes to the nearest integer \(ties to even: round\(2.5\)=2, round\(-2.5\)=-2\); int\(\) cuts off the decimals, i.e. goes toward zero \(int\(-2.7\) = -2\); math.floor\(\) always goes down \(floor\(-2.7\) = -3\). They agree for positive non-tie values like 2.7 only partly \(round 3, int 2, floor 2\).)/Dest[14 0 R/Fit]/Parent 1702 0 R/Prev 1741 0 R/Next 1724 0 R>>
endobj

1743 0 obj
<</Title(Fat 4.2 3.1)/Dest[14 0 R/Fit]/Parent 1725 0 R/Next 1760 0 R>>
endobj

1744 0 obj
<</Title(Three unexpected results demonstrated: \(1\) \(4.2-3.5\)/0.1 is not exactly 7; \(2\) int\(\(3.1-3.5\)/0.1\) gives -3 instead of -4 because int\(\) truncates toward zero; \(3\) round\(2.5\) = 2 and round\(-2.5\) = -2 \(ties go to the even number\), and also -7 // 2 = -4 and -2 ** 2 = -4. Conclusion: round\(\) is the correct choice for step counts. floor\(\) is correct here only by luck \(a value like 6.999999 would give 6\); int\(\) is wrong for negative values. Time: all three are O\(1\) per call \(round 72 ns, int 58 ns, floor 38 ns in my run\) and use O\(1\) space.)/Dest[14 0 R/Fit]/Parent 1725 0 R/Prev 1761 0 R/First 1762 0 R/Last 1763 0 R/Count -2>>
endobj

