<</Title(1. input\(\) returns a string because the keyboard only produces characters; Python cannot know whether '8' is a number, a name or an ID, so it always returns text and the programmer converts explicitly with int\(\) or float\(\). 2. int\('8'\) = 8, float\('8.5'\) = 8.5; int\('8.5'\) raises ValueError \(shown above\). 3. Invalid inputs: '8,5' \(comma instead of decimal point\) and 'eight' both fail float\(\) with ValueError and are rejected. 4. Validation: either check the text before converting \(strip, then check that it contains only digits and at most one '.'\), or convert and catch the error; after conversion also check the range \(litres >= 0, fat 2.0-7.0\).)/Dest[15 0 R/Fit]/Parent 1783 0 R/First 1832 0 R/Last 1833 0 R/Count -3/Next 1809 0 R>>
endobj

1809 0 obj
<</Title(5. try / except ValueError: read_float\(\) above keeps asking until float\(\) succeeds. Note: float\(\) also accepts '1e2', '-3', 'nan' and 'inf', so range checks \(and a math.isfinite test, used in calculator.py in Task 9\) are still needed. 6. f-string formatting: {amount:8.2f} = width 8, 2 decimals, right-aligned \(output above shows | 283.90|\).)/Dest[16 0 R/Fit]/Parent 1783 0 R/Prev 1808 0 R>>
